{
  "abstract": "A smaller Collatz source reaching a prescribed endpoint may need both extra odd depth and extra even steps. We separate these resources for prefixes of the repeated odd exponent word $(1,2,1,2,1,2,2)$. If the original source is divisible by $3^k$, every smaller odd source at an $r$-step endpoint requires more than $r+k$ odd steps. Even with unrestricted alternative odd depth, its extra even-shortcut cost $B$ satisfies $7673\\,2^{k+B}>556\\,3^k$. The proof combines an earlier matched-depth minimum theorem with a new finite criterion coupling positive and negative rational shadows. An independent checker verifies its complete certificate of 24 positive seeds and 104 negative numerator paths. We also give explicit smaller sources on arithmetic classes for every $k$, with additional odd depth $k+q_*(k)$ and even cost $k+1$, where $q_*(k)$ is the least integer satisfying $2^{2k+q+1}<3^{k+q}$. These meetings occur before any original descent. Replacing part of the construction by a known terminating word saves divisions and admits additional input classes. Nevertheless, the entire terminating-seed compiler has conditional natural density below $15/32$ at each exact ternary valuation, and these densities tend to zero. An original-source height bound justifies the passage from ternary Haar measure to natural density for the infinite seed union. The constructions retain their exact domains and physical clocks; neither they nor the exclusions give a universal source selector or a proof of the Collatz conjecture.",
